WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.761 --> 00:00:15.295
Hi, Little Fox readers! It's me, Mellissa, again.

2
00:00:15.319 --> 00:00:23.520
My little brother just asked me to take a picture of his plastic army soldiers. He has them lined up outside in straight long rows.

3
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:32.260
They look kind of like the cool landmark Mr. Fay told us about this week: the Terra-cotta Army in China's Shaanxi province.

4
00:00:32.261 --> 00:00:39.908
The Terra-cotta soldiers are a lot bigger than my brother's plastic toys, though. They're life-size and made of clay.

5
00:00:39.909 --> 00:00:52.256
In fact, this ancient massive army is considered one of the world's greatest archaeological discoveries and was even declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987.

6
00:00:52.257 --> 00:00:58.246
The Terra-cotta Army was created during the reign of Emperor Qin Shihuangdi.

7
00:00:58.247 --> 00:01:10.815
In 221 B.C., Qin Shihuangdi—who was just a king at the time—managed to conquer and unify China's warring states and proclaim himself China's first emperor.

8
00:01:10.816 --> 00:01:16.965
He was a brutal leader who had lots of people executed and trusted almost no one.

9
00:01:16.966 --> 00:01:20.391
But he also accomplished some pretty impressive things.

10
00:01:20.392 --> 00:01:29.126
He standardized the area's weights and measures, laws, written language, weapons—even the length of axles on carts!

11
00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:38.202
Under his orders new fortresses were constructed, along with a series of defensive walls that eventually became the Great Wall of China.

12
00:01:38.202 --> 00:01:44.382
In addition, he instructed workers to build an underground funerary compound for himself.

13
00:01:44.383 --> 00:01:47.958
That's where he would be buried after death.

14
00:01:47.959 --> 00:01:55.260
Mr. Fay told us that Qin Shihuangdi's tomb was huge—about fifty square kilometers!

15
00:01:55.261 --> 00:02:07.017
According to a famous historian, it contained a small-scale model of the world with miniature palaces, trees carved from jade, even flowing rivers and an ocean.

16
00:02:07.018 --> 00:02:13.445
Mercury was used for the water, and pearls were attached to the ceiling to represent stars.

17
00:02:13.446 --> 00:02:20.928
In order to protect the tomb from robbers, workers rigged crossbows that would automatically fire if anyone entered.

18
00:02:20.929 --> 00:02:32.331
Before Qin Shihuangdi's reign, it was customary for a ruler's entire royal court to be slaughtered and buried with him, so that they could continue to serve him in the afterlife.

19
00:02:32.332 --> 00:02:41.156
However, this practice had long been abandoned by the emperor's time, so he decided to use clay statues instead.

20
00:02:41.157 --> 00:02:51.334
Qin Shihuangdi also ordered his workers to build a clay army that could guard his tomb after he died—this became the Terra-cotta army.

21
00:02:51.335 --> 00:03:03.975
The army includes a huge assortment of soldiers—at least eight thousand infantry, cavalry, archers, officers—all in battle formation and organized according to military rank.

22
00:03:03.975 --> 00:03:12.364
The statues' bodies are pretty similar, but each warrior has unique details such as hats, mustaches, shoes, and hair.

23
00:03:12.364 --> 00:03:18.051
While some of the warriors look fierce and ready for battle, others appear pretty calm.

24
00:03:18.052 --> 00:03:23.972
They were originally armed with real bronze weapons like swords, spears, and axes.

25
00:03:23.973 --> 00:03:29.192
Some are even crouching, ready to fire the working crossbows they once held.

26
00:03:29.216 --> 00:03:34.770
In addition to the warriors, there are also life-size horses and chariots.

27
00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:39.712
Some scholars have assumed that the statues are portraits of actual soldiers.

28
00:03:39.713 --> 00:03:47.528
It's more likely, however, that they don't represent specific people, but rather, all the various ethnic groups in China.

29
00:03:47.529 --> 00:03:54.820
Approximately one thousand craftsmen worked for 11 years to make the statues from terra-cotta, a type of clay.

30
00:03:54.821 --> 00:04:04.999
All the soldiers' legs were cast in similar molds, but for the upper bodies and arms, different types of molds were used, to vary the soldiers' head and body shapes.

31
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:15.723
After the body sections were pulled out of the molds, expert sculptors refined them, adding all sorts of minute details to make the statues look unique and lifelike.

32
00:04:15.724 --> 00:04:21.302
And get this: every warrior has the name of the sculptor who made it.

33
00:04:21.303 --> 00:04:25.402
At first I assumed the artists were proud of their work and wanted to sign it.

34
00:04:25.403 --> 00:04:30.465
But Mr. Fay said they really did it so their work could be checked for quality.

35
00:04:30.466 --> 00:04:42.302
The emperor insisted that any statue that wasn't perfect had to be redone, or worse, he would have the sculptor—gulp—executed! Talk about pressure!

36
00:04:42.303 --> 00:04:49.626
After all the carving was finished, the statues were dried in a kiln, and then coated in a thick layer of lacquer.

37
00:04:49.627 --> 00:04:53.622
Next workers painted them in rich, bright colors.

38
00:04:53.623 --> 00:05:00.112
The finished statues were carried down ramps to narrow underground halls, where they were put into position.

39
00:05:00.113 --> 00:05:04.198
Then the halls were sealed off and hidden from view.

40
00:05:04.199 --> 00:05:08.933
For over two thousand years, nobody saw the Terra-cotta Army.

41
00:05:08.934 --> 00:05:15.527
In fact, it wasn't mentioned in historical texts, so nobody even knew it existed.

42
00:05:15.528 --> 00:05:23.011
Then in 1974 some peasants stumbled across fragments of a clay statue in the ground while digging a well.

43
00:05:23.012 --> 00:05:32.251
Government archaeologists began excavating the site, and discovered an enormous underground pit, containing thousands of the clay soldiers.

44
00:05:32.252 --> 00:05:37.573
Eventually more pits were found, each containing many soldiers and horses.

45
00:05:37.574 --> 00:05:43.079
Excavation has continued at the site ever since the discovery of the Terra-cotta Army.

46
00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:50.912
The first three pits that were found have been enclosed in large buildings and are now the Museum of the Terracotta Army.

47
00:05:50.913 --> 00:05:56.252
What's really cool about this museum is that it's still a functioning archaeological site.

48
00:05:56.253 --> 00:06:01.902
So visitors to the museum can actually see the excavation process.

49
00:06:01.903 --> 00:06:07.072
Unfortunately it hasn't been easy to safely dig up the Terra-cotta Army.

50
00:06:07.073 --> 00:06:15.471
When a statue is exposed to the air, the lacquer on its surface immediately shrinks and peels away from the clay, taking the pigment with it.

51
00:06:15.472 --> 00:06:23.129
Archaeologists unearthing these statues sometimes have just minutes to try to preserve the color before it's gone forever.

52
00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:28.896
Because of this and other difficulties, the excavation has been a very slow process;

53
00:06:28.896 --> 00:06:36.707
as of 2012 archaeologists hadn't done any excavation of the tomb itself—they're afraid they might damage it.

54
00:06:36.708 --> 00:06:44.875
Well, I have to get going now. My brother wants me to take that picture of his army before he knocks it down with his remote control army tank!

55
00:06:44.876 --> 00:06:48.023
See you next week!
